# Cloudflare Workers static-asset response headers.
#
# ONE rule per concern, never two rules setting the same header name. A request that matches
# several rules inherits ALL of their headers, so a catch-all "/*" Cache-Control rule alongside the
# one below would ADD a second, conflicting directive rather than override it. How a duplicate name
# is then represented on the wire is deliberately not stated here: Cloudflare documents the values
# as comma-joined, a local `wrangler dev` run returned two separate header lines, and nothing in
# this file depends on which one the edge does.
#
# HTML is deliberately left on the Cloudflare default (public, max-age=0, must-revalidate): every
# route's index.html is regenerated on each deploy behind an unchanged URL, so it must revalidate.
# Only /_framework/* is safe to freeze, because every file in it is fingerprinted.
#
# css/*.css and favicon.png are NOT frozen: their static web asset fingerprints are "soft" (the
# published physical name stays bare), so an immutable rule there would pin a mutable URL for a year.

/_framework/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Frozen for the same reason /_framework/* is, reached differently. The soft fingerprints described
# above are why css/*.css is not frozen; a font file instead carries the font's OWN version in its
# name (geist-1.800, jetbrains-mono-2.211), so replacing one necessarily changes its URL and pinning
# the old one for a year is harmless. Rename the file when you replace the font; never reuse a name.
#
# A separate path pattern, not a widened /_framework/* one: matching rules MERGE, so a single
# pattern covering both would be the duplicate-header case the note at the top of this file warns
# about.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# /404 is reachable as an ordinary 200 page, not only as a 404 response body: with html_handling at
# its default "auto-trailing-slash", Workers serves 404.html for "/404" with a 200 and redirects
# "/404.html" to "/404". A 404 STATUS response is already non-indexable; a 200 one is not, hence
# this rule. Both halves are documented, and site/wrangler.jsonc writes html_handling out rather
# than relying on the default.
#
# CI appends a "/*" noindex rule to preview deploys, and it changes nothing observable: Cloudflare
# puts its own X-Robots-Tag: noindex on a preview URL, and that replaces whatever this file sets for
# that header name (measured on pr-340). The merge described at the top still governs every OTHER
# header, including the two Cache-Control rules above, which do arrive on a preview.
/404
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
